// Proposed sections for patches/SLUS-20973_4028A55F.pnach (docs/issues/native-soak.md,
// findings 3 and 2; "Save point" and "Script variables" there).  Not in the patch file and
// not in the suggested list: the set changes only at a release.

[Net test: save point is used on every machine whatever this machine's card screen does]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the difference on native pairs (docs/issues/native-soak.md, section "Save point"); untested on the emulator, PCSX2 or a console
description=When a player presses use at a save point, every machine runs the save point's own code inside the tick (Savepoint_interact, 0x28DC30) - but a machine whose own memory-card screen is up at that moment (its save dialog, the options being written after a change in the pause menu) skips all of it, so the save point's state (+0x84, its light, its active flag and, were there any, its action scripts) then differs from the other machines': the "saved" burst, the point's private random numbers and the 6 bytes it writes into the level's stored state follow on some machines and not on others. This swaps the two tests and narrows the card one: the menu-mutex test (shared state in a net game) still guards everything, the card test guards only the request to open this machine's save dialog. Nothing else changes; a machine that was not busy does exactly what it did. Offline the only change is that the point lights up when used while a card screen is open. FINDINGS B9. Each machine fixes its own copy; use on every machine.
// was: 0C085D46 (jal memcard_isBusy)  now: jal menuMutex_isHeld
patch=1,EE,0028DC44,word,0C08BF0B
// was: 0C08BF0B (jal menuMutex_isHeld)  now: jal memcard_isBusy
patch=1,EE,0028DC54,word,0C085D46
// was: 1440000A (bnez v0,0x28dc88)  now: bnez v0,0x28dc68
patch=1,EE,0028DC5C,word,14400002
// was: 24100001 (addiu s0,zero,1)  now: jal memcard_requestSave
patch=1,EE,0028DC64,word,0C085D6D
// was: 0C085D6D (jal memcard_requestSave)  now: addiu s0,zero,1
patch=1,EE,0028DC68,word,24100001

[Net test: reading a quest variable never adds it to the table]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the difference on a native pair (docs/issues/native-soak.md, section "Script variables"); untested on the emulator, PCSX2 or a console
description=Reading a quest variable that does not exist yet (script getv, vars_get 0x1ED778) appends its name to the table with value 0. Level scripts are also run from drawing code - the prompt of a jump gate or a locked object for this machine's own player, the marker over a character's head, the map - so the machine that draws appends the name some ticks before the others, or alone, and the tables then differ in content or in order. This makes a read of a missing name return 0 and leave the table as it is; names are added only by a store (setv), which every machine does in the same tick. Every value read is what it was (a missing name read 0 before, too); the table and the save file hold only variables that were ever set. docs/script.md P1. Per machine; a mix is as safe as the game is without it.
// was: 0C0EE1A3 (jal libc_strcpy)  now: b 0x1ed844 (v0 is 0 on both ways in)
patch=1,EE,001ED828,word,10000006

[Net test: a jump gate is found only inside the tick]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the difference on a native pair (docs/issues/native-soak.md, section "Script variables"); untested on the emulator, PCSX2 or a console
description=The prompt of a jump gate (JumpGate_interactPrompt, 0x27D7A0) marks a gate nobody has found yet as found (+0x4D), adds the level to the list of jump-gate destinations (jumpgate_addCurrentLevel) and shows the "activated" message. The prompt runs inside the tick for every player, but also from drawing for this machine's own player, where the tests in front of it (menu open, camera flag) use this machine's current state and not the lockstep one: a player who closes the inventory beside an unfound gate has the gate found and the destination added on the owner's machine as many ticks early as its input runs ahead (19 ticks in the directed game), and for good if the player dies or the level changes before the tick gets there. This makes the prompt mark the gate only when it was called from the tick: Player_interact keeps its display-only argument in register s7 across the call (0x1B2650; the prompt has no other caller but the reflector's forwarding prompt, which leaves s7 alone), and the test "already found" becomes "already found, or called for display". The prompt's text and result at draw time are those of a found gate. Per machine; a mix is as safe as the game is without it.
// was: 14400010 (bnez v0,0x27d864)  now: or v0,v0,s7
patch=1,EE,0027D820,word,00571025
// was: 3C02008E (lui v0,0x8e)  now: bnez v0,0x27d860 (which loads the same v0; the addiu v0,zero,1 behind it is now the delay slot and is overwritten there)
patch=1,EE,0027D824,word,1440000E
